<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=chinampa">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinampa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as chinampas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A chinampa is an artificial agricultural plot built up from mud and vegetation in shallow lake beds, separated by canals. The chinampas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> produced several harvests a year. They were a major source of food for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinampa.jpg" alt="Chinampa" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Weule, †1926 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinampas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1150</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Valley of Mexico</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Culhuacan may have built the first chinampas about 1100; the Aztecs did not invent them but farmed them on a far larger scale.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize<span>c. 7000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=chinampa">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
